**Tamarin** sits between Flic en Flac and Black River around a bay framed by the mountains of the west. It feels more like a village people live in than a resort. Mauritian families and people from abroad who live here all year and a tight surf community share the same streets.

## The bay and its surf

Tamarin Bay was once called Santosha Bay and became known far beyond Mauritius after the 1974 surf film *Forgotten Island of Santosha*. It has two classic breaks: "Dal" on the south side of the bay and "Black Stone" to the north. Waves depend on the swell and the season. This is a spot for experienced surfers with a strong local lineup. Arrive with respect.

## Dolphins

Spinner and bottlenose dolphins are often seen in the bay early in the morning before they head back to the open sea. Boat trips leave from Tamarin and neighbouring Black River. Choose an operator that keeps its distance and avoids crowding the animals: the bay is where the dolphins rest.

## The salt pans

Beside the coast road lie the Salines de Tamarin which for a long time were the island's main source of salt. Seawater was pumped into shallow basins and left to evaporate in the sun. The method dates back to the French colonial period. Much of the production has since stopped in the face of cheaper imported salt.

## Getting there

Tamarin is a few kilometres south of Flic en Flac along the coast road and roughly an hour by car from the international airport.

## Good to know

- The bay is where the river meets the sea so the water can be less clear after heavy rain.
- For the best light on the mountains come in the late afternoon.
